  <Section id="question" num="01" label="The question" title="A machine that vanishes without warning.">
    <p>
      Free-tier GPUs are preempted without draining: no warning, no checkpoint, no disk. A pipeline
      that spans machines therefore has to treat failure as routine rather than exceptional. Most
      pipeline-parallel systems assume nodes are stable and route around the rare failure. On
      free-tier hardware the failure is the steady state.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question here is narrow. Not <em>can</em> a model be split across machines — that is
      settled — but what recovery actually costs when a machine disappears mid-token, in latency,
      in recomputed work, and in correctness.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="system" num="02" label="The system" title="Contiguous blocks, one fixed envelope.">
    <p>
      The model is split into contiguous layer blocks, one per worker. A client holds the embedding
      and the un-embedding, so every hop carries a hidden state of the same shape and the final GPU
      does not need the vocabulary projection resident.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each activation travels in a fixed-size envelope: a 43-byte header followed by a raw,
      dtype-native payload. The header carries a request id, a monotonic hop counter, the exact
      layer to resume from, the layer to stop after, the tensor shape and dtype, and a CRC-32. A
      receiver validates the header before allocating anything, so a truncated or out-of-order
      message is rejected cheaply.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hop counter doubles as a logical clock: a duplicate hop number means a replayed envelope,
      a jump of two means a skipped stage, and an exhausted counter means a cycle. Layers may
      overlap between neighbouring workers; the overlap is what makes mid-token failover
      structurally possible, because a replacement must already own the layer where the pipeline
      stopped.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="experiment" num="03" label="The experiment" title="Same failure, two recovery strategies.">
    <p>
      Two modes, same failure, same model. <strong>Boundary resume</strong> replays from the last
      completed block boundary, which is what a re-routing scheduler does by default.
      <strong>Exact-position resume</strong> replays from the token position where the worker died,
      using the checkpointed hidden state and KV cache.
    </p>
    <p>
      A worker is killed at a random token position, {ph('N')} times, with the same prompt and
      sampling settings. Each run records four metrics: time-to-next-token, recomputed work, resume
      rate, and output equivalence. The first is what the user feels. The second is the price of the
      strategy. The third proves it is a property rather than a lucky run. The fourth proves replay
      is correct, not merely fast.
    </p>
  </Section>

