  <Section id="limits" num="06" label="Known limits" title="Named prior art, stated plainly.">
    <p>
      Layer-split pipeline inference is not new. Petals runs it across volunteer peers, exo
      partitions models across everyday devices, and llama.cpp's RPC backend and distributed-llama
      do it on a LAN. PipeDream's 1F1B schedule is the datacenter version of the same recovery
      problem. What differs here is the operating condition and the measurement: free-tier GPUs are
      preempted without draining, so the question is not whether a pipeline can span machines, but
      what recovery costs when a machine vanishes mid-token. I do not claim a new architecture. I
      claim a measured failure regime.
    </p>
    <Limits>
      <p>
        This setup cannot show public-swarm scale, adversarial peers, long-running stability, or
        throughput on a healthy cluster. It is three operator-controlled workers, run briefly, with
        fault injection triggered deliberately — which is also why it does not run a public endpoint:
        free Colab is intended for interactive use, and a persistent inference service for third
        parties would breach its acceptable-use policy.
      </p>
    </Limits>
  </Section>

